// Pocket — top-level integration. The only file the host talks to.
//
// A transliteration of the reference emulator's core/machine.ts. That file owns
// a CPU + MMU + PPU + APU + Timer and steps them together; this one does the
// same, except that Pocket has no objects — every device is a set of functions
// over the typed arrays declared in gb_state.logic, so "owning" a device here
// means knowing the ORDER to call it in and nothing else.
//
// Everything host-facing lives here, per CONTRACT.md §3.8: reset, ROM loading,
// stepping, the two wire-format encoders (§7.1 PixelCanvas, §7.2 AudioStream),
// the joypad entry point, and save states.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE INNER LOOP
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// CONTRACT.md §3.8 fixes it:
//
//   while (budget > 0) {
//     let c = gbCpu_serviceIrq()
//     if (c === 0) { c = gbCpu_step() }
//     gbMachine_tickParts(c)
//     budget = budget - c
//   }
//
// `budget` is GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] and it is deliberately SIGNED and
// PERSISTENT. An instruction cannot be cut in half, so a slice almost always
// overshoots — the last instruction of a 70224-cycle frame drags the budget a
// few cycles below zero. Carrying that remainder into the next slice is what
// keeps a long run at exactly 70224 T-cycles per frame on average instead of
// gaining up to twenty cycles a frame, which is four whole scanlines a minute.
//
// It is persistent BETWEEN calls, not within one: gbMachine_runSlice keeps it
// in a local for the length of the loop and writes it back on every exit, which
// is what the array slot is for and costs three Int32Array accesses an
// instruction less than reading it back each time round.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TICK ORDER
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// gbMachine_tickParts is the one place in the bundle that knows the order, and
// the order is load-bearing:
//
//   1. gbTimer_step       — can raise the timer IRQ; must land before the PPU
//                           step that would otherwise race it to the same
//                           M-cycle.
//   2. gbPpu_step         — drives the mode machine, and on the mode 3 -> 0
//                           edge calls gbMmu_hblankHdma().
//   3. gbApu_step         — samples off the same clock; nothing depends on it.
//   4. gbMmu_stepOamDma   — last, so a DMA byte written this M-cycle is visible
//                           to the next instruction and not to this one.
//
// Peripherals are ticked BETWEEN instructions, not per memory access, and in
// 4-cycle (1 M-cycle) chunks whenever any of them has something to do inside
// the instruction. When none of them does — which is 87% of instructions on a
// DMG game and 97% on one that spends its frame halted — the whole instruction
// is taken in one hop by the quiet span at the top of gbMachine_tickParts —
// where the order above stops mattering, because by construction none of the
// four does anything over that span.
//
// The reference's per-M-cycle `tickM` machinery is out of scope
// (CONTRACT.md §9); chunking at M-cycle granularity keeps the timer and the
// STAT line honest inside a long instruction, which is what the frame counter
// and every raster effect actually depend on, and gives back the large share of
// the CPU budget that a per-access rewrite would cost.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// ALLOCATION
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Per CONTRACT.md §1.5 the only per-frame allocations in the whole bundle are
// the two result objects returned by gbMachine_getFrame() and
// gbMachine_getSamples(), and the two base64 strings inside them. They are
// unavoidable: a typed array reads back as null across the host boundary, so
// the picture and the PCM have to be strings. Nothing else here allocates.
// The save-state path does allocate a working buffer, and is allowed to — it is
// a cold path that runs when a human presses a button, not per frame.

// ===========================================================================
// Constants
// ===========================================================================

// CONTRACT.md §3.8: advanceFrame gives up after two frames' worth of cycles.
// This is a cap on what ONE CALL may spend, never an amount added to the
// budget — see the note on leakage above gbMachine_advanceFrame.
let GB_MACHINE_FRAME_LIMIT = 140448

// CONTRACT.md §3.8: more than five million T-cycles in one slice is not a slow
// game, it is a loop that will never end. Mirrors the reference's runaway check.
let GB_MACHINE_RUNAWAY = 5000000

// GB_FCTR slots 5 and 6 are unnamed in gb_state.logic. They are claimed here
// rather than there because they are not machine state — they are the wall
// clock the FPS readout is measured against, which belongs to whoever drives
// the machine. Named, per the spirit of the GB_CTR spare-index rule, so the
// next module to want a double does not silently take the same slot.
let GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_T0 = 5
let GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N = 6

// The FPS window. Long enough that a single slow frame does not make the
// number jump, short enough that the reading tracks a stall.
let GB_MACHINE_FPS_WINDOW_MS = 500

// Save-state container. "PKS" + version byte, then a fixed section order.
let GB_MACHINE_STATE_VERSION = 1
let GB_MACHINE_STATE_HEADER = 32

// ===========================================================================
// Init and reset
// ===========================================================================

// Once, ever. Builds the two 256-entry dispatch tables and clears the machine.
// Idempotent: a second call is a single array-length test, so every entry point
// that could plausibly run first is free to call it.
function gbMachine_init() {
  if (GB_OPS.length !== 0) { return }
  gbOps_buildTables()
  gbState_reset()
  gbMachine_resetFpsClock()
  gbStatus = "No cartridge"
}

// Power cycle. Keeps the cartridge (and its battery RAM, and the user's palette
// choice); wipes everything else and applies the post-boot register set.
function gbMachine_reset() {
  gbMachine_init()
  gbState_reset()
  gbMachine_pacingStalls = 0

  // gbState_reset does not touch GB_CART, because the cartridge's identity —
  // mapper, sizes, masks — outlives a reset. Its BANK REGISTERS do not: a
  // machine that came up with bank 7 still mapped would fetch its reset vector
  // out of the wrong half of the ROM. gb_cart has no reset of its own in
  // CONTRACT.md §3.1, so the power-on values are restored here and the offsets
  // recomputed through the owner's gbCart_remap().
  if (GB_ROM !== null) {
    GB_CART[GB_K_BANKLO] = 1
    GB_CART[GB_K_BANKHI] = 0
    GB_CART[GB_K_MODE] = 0
    GB_CART[GB_K_RAMEN] = 0
    GB_CART[GB_K_RAMBANK] = 0
    GB_RTC[GB_RTC_SEL] = 0
    gbCart_remap()
  }

  // 0x80 = CGB + DMG, 0xC0 = CGB only; anything else is a DMG cart. Read back
  // from GB_CART rather than GB_CPUF because gbState_reset just cleared the
  // latter — gbCpu_reset is what puts GB_P_GBC back.
  let flag = GB_CART[GB_K_CGBFLAG]
  let gbc = flag === 0x80 || flag === 0xc0

  gbCpu_reset(gbc)
  gbApu_reset()
  gbMachine_postBootIo()

  // So the very first getFrame() has a palette to hand back even if it lands
  // before the first VBlank.
  gbPpu_buildPalette()

  GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] = 0
  gbMachine_resetFpsClock()
  gbFrameCounter = 0
  gbFps = 0
  gbGbcMode = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] === 1
  if (gbRomLoaded) { gbStatus = "Running" }
}

// Pocket starts post-boot (CONTRACT.md §9), so the values a boot ROM would have
// left in the IO space have to be put there by hand. These are the reference
// emulator's own field defaults — ppu.ts declares lcdc = 0x91 and bgp = 0xfc —
// written through the owning module's writeReg rather than poked into GB_IO,
// per the ownership rule in CONTRACT.md §5.
//
// STAT is deliberately absent: its only writable bits (3-6) are zero in the
// post-boot 0x85, so writing it would be a no-op, and gb_ppu derives the rest.
// The APU is deliberately absent too: the reference starts NR50/NR51 at zero
// and so does gbApu_reset(). Writing the real post-boot NR14 = 0xBF would
// re-trigger channel 1 and reproduce the boot chime as a click on every reset,
// and the register READS back as 0xBF either way because only bit 6 is
// readable.
function gbMachine_postBootIo() {
  gbPpu_writeReg(0xff40, 0x91)
  gbPpu_writeReg(0xff47, 0xfc)
  gbPpu_writeReg(0xff48, 0xff)
  gbPpu_writeReg(0xff49, 0xff)
  // Both select lines high — nothing selected, which is what the boot ROM
  // leaves behind and what makes a P1 read return 0xCF with no buttons down.
  gbMmu_writeP1(0x30)
}

function gbMachine_resetFpsClock() {
  GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_T0] = 0
  GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N] = 0
  GB_FCTR[GB_F_FPS] = 0
}

// ===========================================================================
// Cartridge loading
// ===========================================================================

// Uint8Array.fromBase64 is bounded: one call decodes at most 96 KB before the
// engine stops it with "native builtin iteration limit exceeded" — a guard on
// how long a native builtin may run without returning to the interpreter, not
// a statement about the input. Game Boy cartridges start at 32 KB and run to
// 8 MB, and MBC5 battery RAM reaches 128 KB, so nearly every real cartridge
// crossed it and was reported as corrupt.
//
// Decoding in pieces stays under the limit. The boundary has to fall on a
// multiple of 4 characters: base64 carries 3 bytes per 4 characters, and a
// split anywhere else makes each piece decode with padding the whole never
// had. 65536 is a multiple of 4 and decodes to 48 KB, half the ceiling.
let GB_B64_CHUNK_CHARS = 65536

// Consecutive advanceFrame calls the pacing guard has deferred with no drain in
// between. The limit is in rAF calls, so it has to sit above the longest a
// HEALTHY audio path can leave the ring full: the ring holds 8192 stereo frames,
// about 170ms, and a tab returning from the background drains that in ~10 calls
// at 60Hz. Thirty is half a second — never reached while audio is really
// playing, and a dead audio path is noticed well before anyone reads 0 FPS.
let GB_PACING_STALL_LIMIT = 30
let gbMachine_pacingStalls = 0

function gbMachine_decodeBase64(payload) {
  if (payload.length <= GB_B64_CHUNK_CHARS) { return Uint8Array.fromBase64(payload) }

  let parts = []
  let total = 0
  let at = 0
  while (at < payload.length) {
    let end = at + GB_B64_CHUNK_CHARS
    if (end > payload.length) { end = payload.length }
    let piece = Uint8Array.fromBase64(payload.slice(at, end))
    parts.push(piece)
    total = total + piece.length
    at = end
  }

  let out = new Uint8Array(total)
  let offset = 0
  let i = 0
  while (i < parts.length) {
    out.set(parts[i], offset)
    offset = offset + parts[i].length
    i = i + 1
  }
  return out
}

// The host path. Typed arrays read back as null across the boundary
// (CONTRACT.md §1.8), so a ROM arrives as base64 — from a file input, a fetch,
// or a data: URL, all three of which are accepted.
function gbMachine_loadRomBase64(s) {
  gbMachine_init()
  gbError = ""

  if (typeof s !== "string" || s.length === 0) {
    gbError = "No ROM data was supplied"
    gbStatus = "No cartridge"
    return false
  }

  let payload = s
  let marker = s.indexOf("base64,")
  if (marker >= 0) { payload = s.slice(marker + 7) }

  let bytes = null
  try {
    bytes = gbMachine_decodeBase64(payload)
  } catch (error) {
    // Say what actually went wrong. This reported "not valid base64" for
    // every failure, so a cartridge that was too big to decode in one call
    // looked exactly like a corrupt file.
    gbError = "Could not decode the ROM: " + error
    gbStatus = "No cartridge"
    return false
  }

  return gbMachine_loadRom(bytes)
}

// The in-VM path, for anything that already holds the bytes — the base64
// entry point above, a test harness, a future bundled ROM.
function gbMachine_loadRom(bytes) {
  gbMachine_init()
  gbError = ""

  if (bytes === null || bytes === undefined || bytes.length < 0x150) {
    gbError = "That file is too small to be a cartridge ROM"
    gbRomLoaded = false
    gbRunning = false
    gbStatus = "No cartridge"
    return false
  }

  if (!gbCart_load(bytes)) {
    // gbCart_load has already put the reason in gbError.
    gbRomLoaded = false
    gbRunning = false
    gbStatus = "Cartridge rejected"
    return false
  }

  gbRomLoaded = true
  gbMachine_reset()

  gbTitle = gbCart_title()
  gbMapperName = gbMachine_mapperName()
  gbGbcMode = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] === 1
  gbRunning = true
  gbStatus = gbTitle === "" ? "Running" : gbTitle
  return true
}

function gbMachine_mapperName() {
  let mbc = GB_CART[GB_K_MBC]
  if (mbc === GB_MBC_NONE) { return "ROM only" }
  if (mbc === GB_MBC_1) { return "MBC1" }
  if (mbc === GB_MBC_3) { return "MBC3" }
  if (mbc === GB_MBC_5) { return "MBC5" }
  return "MBC?"
}

// ===========================================================================
// Stepping
// ===========================================================================

// Peripherals, in the one order that is correct, at M-cycle granularity.
//
// On CGB double speed the CPU clock doubles but the dot clock does not: the
// PPU still draws 70224 dots a frame and the APU still runs its frame sequencer
// off 4.19MHz, while DIV and the OAM DMA are CPU-clocked and keep the full
// count. Instruction lengths are always multiples of four, so halving is exact
// and needs no remainder carried anywhere. In single speed — every DMG game and
// most CGB ones — the branch is not taken and this is byte-for-byte the loop
// CONTRACT.md §3.8 specifies.
// The whole-M-cycle case is the loop; the short tail is handled once at the
// end. Written this way rather than with a min() inside the loop because this
// is the most-called function in the bundle — roughly 17,500 iterations a
// frame — and the version with the per-chunk branch measured 0.400ms/frame
// against 0.368ms here on the native engine with empty devices. Same order,
// same chunking, same tail.
function gbMachine_tickParts(cycles) {
  if (gbCpu_double === 1) {
    gbMachine_tickPartsDouble(cycles)
    return
  }

  // Reached only when some device has an event inside this instruction. The
  // quiet span that covers the other ninety-odd instructions in a hundred is
  // inline in gbMachine_runSlice, where it costs no call; see THE QUIET SPAN
  // there. The test it made is repeated by the caller, so arriving here means
  // the chunked walk below is the right thing.
  //
  // gbMmu_stepOamDma returns immediately unless a serial transfer or an OAM
  // DMA is actually in flight, and neither is true for the overwhelming
  // majority of a frame. Hoisting its own early-out to the caller turns 17556
  // calls per frame into 17556 compares against zero.
  //
  // This is worth the ugliness: a call into the VM costs ~86ns on the shipping
  // engine, so a function that does nothing still costs 1.5ms per frame out of
  // a 16.67ms budget — measured, not estimated. The condition is exactly the
  // pair of tests gbMmu_stepOamDma opens with, so the behaviour is identical.
  // The test is re-read every iteration rather than hoisted into a local: the
  // hoisted version would be correct only for as long as nothing inside the
  // loop can arm a transfer, and that is a property of the other modules that
  // a future change could quietly take away.
  let left = cycles
  while (left >= 4) {
    gbTimer_step(4)
    gbPpu_step(4)
    gbApu_step(4)
    if (gbMmu_busy !== 0) {
      gbMmu_stepOamDma(4)
    }
    left = left - 4
  }
  if (left > 0) {
    gbTimer_step(left)
    gbPpu_step(left)
    gbApu_step(left)
    if (gbMmu_busy !== 0) {
      gbMmu_stepOamDma(left)
    }
  }
}

// CGB double speed. Instruction lengths are always multiples of four, so the
// halving is exact in the loop and no remainder is ever carried; the tail
// exists only for a caller that hands over a partial M-cycle.
function gbMachine_tickPartsDouble(cycles) {
  let left = cycles
  while (left >= 4) {
    gbTimer_step(4)
    gbPpu_step(2)
    gbApu_step(2)
    if (gbMmu_busy !== 0) {
      gbMmu_stepOamDma(4)
    }
    left = left - 4
  }
  if (left > 0) {
    let dots = left >>> 1
    gbTimer_step(left)
    if (dots > 0) {
      gbPpu_step(dots)
      gbApu_step(dots)
    }
    gbMmu_stepOamDma(left)
  }
}

// The shared engine behind stepCycles and advanceFrame. `stopOnFrame` of 1
// breaks the moment GB_C_FRAME moves, leaving the unspent budget behind for the
// next call — which is exactly the carry-over that keeps the average frame at
// 70224 cycles.
//
// This loop runs once per instruction — 6,500 to 17,500 times a frame — so
// everything in it was priced per iteration on the shipping engine, where a
// compare-and-branch is ~12ns, a call ~30ns and a statement ~7ns:
//
//   - The budget lives in a LOCAL and is written back to
//     GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] on every exit. Nothing else in the bundle reads
//     that slot while a slice is running.
//   - The frame limit and the runaway guard are folded into the loop bound.
//     "spent >= GB_MACHINE_FRAME_LIMIT" is "budget <= start - LIMIT", and
//     "spent > GB_MACHINE_RUNAWAY" is "budget < start - RUNAWAY", so both ride
//     on the one compare the loop already makes, and spent is start - budget
//     at the exit rather than an add per instruction. Which bound ended the
//     loop is decided afterwards, once; the frame edge wins a tie exactly as
//     it did when the checks were in sequence.
//   - The frame edge is one compare in both modes. GB_PPU_FRAME only ever
//     rises, so "past frameStop" is the edge test when stopOnFrame is set, and
//     with frameStop parked at 2^31-1 it cannot fire.
//   - The "handler reported no cycles" guard is gone: gbCpu_step charges a
//     minimum M-cycle itself, on its last line, and gbCpu_serviceIrq returns 0
//     or 20, so c is never below 4 here.
//   - The no-interrupt path makes one compare, not two.
//   - THE QUIET SPAN, below, is inline rather than a call.
//
// Measured on Pokémon Yellow: the loop shape took a frame from 11.7ms to
// 10.9ms, and the inline span a further 0.5ms, against calling
// gbMachine_tickParts on every instruction.
//
// Every exit below writes the budget back. There is no path out of this
// function that does not.
function gbMachine_runSlice(cycles, stopOnFrame) {
  let budget = GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] + cycles
  let start = budget
  let floor = 0
  let frameStop = 0x7fffffff
  if (stopOnFrame === 1) {
    frameStop = GB_PPU_FRAME
    // The VBlank is not coming — the LCD is off, or the cart has wedged the
    // PPU. Give up after two frames' worth rather than burning the whole
    // budget looking for an edge that does not exist.
    if (start - GB_MACHINE_FRAME_LIMIT > floor) { floor = start - GB_MACHINE_FRAME_LIMIT }
  }
  let runaway = start - GB_MACHINE_RUNAWAY - 1
  if (runaway > floor) { floor = runaway }

  while (budget > floor) {
    // An interrupt dispatch costs 20 T-cycles and replaces the instruction that
    // would otherwise have run this iteration; 0 means IME was clear.
    // gbMmu_irqPending is (IE & IF & 0x1F) kept as a scalar, so the
    // overwhelmingly common "nothing pending" case does not pay for the call.
    let c = 0
    if (gbMmu_irqPending !== 0) {
      c = gbCpu_serviceIrq()
      if (c === 0) { c = gbCpu_step() }
    } else {
      c = gbCpu_step()
    }

    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    // THE QUIET SPAN
    //
    // Chunking exists so that a device which is about to DO something does
    // it on the right M-cycle. When no device is going to do anything for the
    // whole of this instruction, chunking buys nothing and costs four calls
    // per M-cycle.
    //
    // All four devices are pure counters while they are quiet, and each one
    // can say cheaply how far away its next event is:
    //
    //   PPU     GB_PPU_DOTS dots to the next mode boundary, GB_PPU_STATD < 0
    //           for "no STAT delivery armed". Both plain scalars.
    //   APU     renders a batch when gbApu_acc reaches gbApu_trigger, 2796
    //           T-cycles apart — 25 times a frame.
    //   timer   gbTimer_live is 0 exactly when TAC's enable is clear with no
    //           stale high bit and no reload draining, which is when
    //           gbTimer_step does nothing but count DIV up; gbTimer_edgeIn
    //           is the distance to the next edge or reload while it is on.
    //   MMU     gbMmu_busy is 0 when neither the serial stub nor the OAM DMA
    //           is in flight, which is when gbMmu_stepOamDma returns at once.
    //
    // If every one of them clears the whole instruction, the instruction's
    // worth of peripheral time is three adds. Correctness rests on each
    // device being MONOTONIC over the span: DOTS only falls, gbApu_acc and
    // DIV only rise, so an intermediate 4-cycle chunk cannot cross a boundary
    // that the whole span does not. The moment any of them would, this falls
    // through to gbMachine_tickParts and the chunked loop, and nothing
    // changes. Double speed never takes the span: the dot clock and the CPU
    // clock disagree there, and tickParts halves for the PPU and APU.
    //
    // gbTimer_step clears the reload flag at the top of every call, so a span
    // that replaces a run of calls has to clear it too. DIV is only counted
    // on the two arms that prove no edge lands inside the span — the
    // countdown cannot erode, because while the timer is off it is not
    // touched, and while it is on it is bounded by one period and re-derived
    // at every edge.
    //
    // Measured on critter_quest: 12.4ms/frame of chunked stepping became
    // 3.1ms as a call, and inlining the call here is a further 5.7% of a
    // frame on Pokémon Yellow.
    // -------------------------------------------------------------------
    if (gbCpu_double === 0 &&
        GB_PPU_DOTS > c &&
        GB_PPU_STATD <= 0 &&
        gbApu_acc + c < gbApu_trigger &&
        gbMmu_busy === 0) {
      GB_PPU_DOTS = GB_PPU_DOTS - c
      gbApu_acc = gbApu_acc + c
      gbTimer_reloaded = 0
      if (gbTimer_live === 0) {
        gbTimer_div = (gbTimer_div + c) & 0xffff
      } else if (gbTimer_edgeIn > c) {
        gbTimer_edgeIn = gbTimer_edgeIn - c
        gbTimer_div = (gbTimer_div + c) & 0xffff
      } else {
        gbTimer_step(c)
      }
    } else {
      gbMachine_tickParts(c)
    }

    budget = budget - c
    if (GB_PPU_FRAME > frameStop) { break }
  }

  if (budget <= runaway && GB_PPU_FRAME <= frameStop) {
    gbError = "Runaway: the CPU produced more than " + GB_MACHINE_RUNAWAY + " cycles in one slice"
    gbRunning = false
    gbStatus = "Stopped"
    GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] = 0
    return start - budget
  }

  GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] = budget
  return start - budget
}

// Advance n T-cycles. The primitive: it does not consult gbRunning, because the
// caller asking for cycles IS the decision to run.
function gbMachine_stepCycles(n) {
  if (!(n > 0)) { return 0 }
  if (!gbRomLoaded) { return 0 }
  return gbMachine_runSlice(n, 0)
}

// One whole emulated frame, 70224 T-cycles exactly, carry-over included.
// The fixed-slice counterpart to gbMachine_advanceFrame: it does not stop early
// at the VBlank edge and it does not consult the audio ring, so a caller that
// wants deterministic frame-for-frame stepping — a test, a TAS, a headless
// render — gets it. `joypadBits` is the §7.3 mask, and may be omitted to leave
// the pad alone.
function gbMachine_runFrame(joypadBits) {
  if (joypadBits !== undefined && joypadBits !== null) {
    gbMachine_setButtons(joypadBits)
  }
  return gbMachine_stepCycles(GB_CYCLES_PER_FRAME)
}

// The pacing entry point, called from getFrame(). Runs until the PPU says a
// frame finished, or gives up after two frames' worth so a machine with the LCD
// off still makes progress.
//
// It adds ONE frame of cycles, not the two-frame limit, and that distinction is
// the whole correctness of the pacing. The budget persists; adding two frames
// and returning at the one-frame edge leaves the other frame sitting in the
// budget, and the next call adds two more on top. Within a couple of seconds
// the residue is large enough that a single stepCycles() call runs four or five
// frames back to back, the audio ring overruns, and the emulator sprints. Add
// what a frame costs, spend what a frame costs.
//
// Stopping early leaves a bounded residue — at most the part of the current
// frame that had already been drawn when the call arrived — and the next call
// consumes it. That residue is the carry-over doing its job, not a leak.
function gbMachine_advanceFrame() {
  if (!gbRunning) { return 0 }
  if (!gbRomLoaded) { return 0 }

  // CONTRACT.md §3.8 pacing. If the host is calling faster than 59.7275Hz — a
  // 120Hz display, or a tab that just came back from the background — the ring
  // fills and the audio, not the display, becomes the clock. The GB_AG_ACTIVE
  // test matters: with audio muted nothing ever drains the ring, and without it
  // a silent bundle would freeze on its first frame.
  if (GB_APU_G[GB_AG_ACTIVE] === 1 &&
      GB_APU_G[GB_AG_RING_COUNT] > 3 * GB_FCTR[GB_F_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME]) {
    // A full ring that nobody is emptying is not a clock.
    //
    // ACTIVE is set by the FIRST drain and never cleared, so once audio has
    // started, this guard trusts it forever. When the AudioContext is then
    // suspended and the browser refuses to resume it — an OS interruption, a
    // battery-saver policy, an output device that went away, or simply a
    // browser stricter than this one — draining stops, the ring stays full,
    // and every call here returns 0. The picture froze at 0 FPS while the
    // machine sat idle, and it looked like the emulator had become 750x
    // slower on that device. Reproduced on an x86 desktop by suspending the
    // context with resume() stubbed out: 60 FPS to 0.0 in one step.
    //
    // A drain resets this counter. If the ring has been full with no drain
    // for GB_PACING_STALL_LIMIT consecutive calls, audio has stopped being a
    // clock: hand it back to the display and let the ring overrun (it drops
    // the oldest frame, bounded). The next real drain sets ACTIVE again and
    // the guard re-arms — the existing lifecycle, just no longer one-way.
    gbMachine_pacingStalls = gbMachine_pacingStalls + 1
    if (gbMachine_pacingStalls <= GB_PACING_STALL_LIMIT) {
      return 0
    }
    GB_APU_G[GB_AG_ACTIVE] = 0
  }
  gbMachine_pacingStalls = 0

  return gbMachine_runSlice(GB_CYCLES_PER_FRAME, 1)
}

// ===========================================================================
// Host wire formats
// ===========================================================================

// CONTRACT.md §7.1. The encoder on its own, with no side effects on the
// machine, so a caller that has already advanced (or deliberately has not) can
// re-encode the same frame.
//
// GB_FB_PRESENT, never the live GB_FB: the host's rAF and the emulated
// 59.7275Hz drift past each other continuously, so about once every couple of
// seconds a getFrame() lands mid-scanout, and encoding the live buffer would
// hand PixelCanvas a picture that is half this frame and half the last.
function gbMachine_encodeFrame() {
  return {
    w: GB_SCREEN_W,
    h: GB_SCREEN_H,
    format: "p8",
    pixels: GB_FB_PRESENT.toBase64(),
    palette: GB_PAL_RGB.toBase64(),
    paletteRev: GB_CTR[GB_C_PAL_REV],
    frame: GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME],
    running: gbRunning
  }
}

// What <PixelCanvas> calls once per requestAnimationFrame: advance, then encode.
function gbMachine_getFrame() {
  let before = GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME]
  gbMachine_advanceFrame()
  let drew = GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME] !== before

  gbFrameCounter = GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME]
  gbMachine_measureFps(drew)

  return gbMachine_encodeFrame()
}

// CONTRACT.md §7.2. `frames` is what gbApu_drain() actually produced, which is
// never assumed and never padded — AudioStream schedules exactly this many.
//
// GB_AUDIO_OUT_U8 is a byte view over the SAME buffer as GB_AUDIO_OUT_I16, so
// this is a base64 of the PCM in place: no copy, no endian pass. Int16Array is
// native-endian and every target is little-endian, hence "s16le".
function gbMachine_encodeSamples(frames) {
  let pcm = ""
  if (frames > 0) {
    pcm = GB_AUDIO_OUT_U8.subarray(0, frames * 4).toBase64()
  }
  return {
    rate: GB_FCTR[GB_F_HOST_RATE],
    channels: 2,
    format: "s16le",
    frames: frames,
    pcm: pcm,
    underruns: GB_APU_G[GB_AG_UNDERRUNS]
  }
}

// What <AudioStream> calls, on its own schedule.
//
// Audio is the clock, not the display. getFrame advances too, and while the
// browser holds 60Hz that is what keeps the picture smooth — but rAF is a
// best-effort timer and the AudioContext is a real one. When a frame costs more
// than 16.7ms, or the tab is throttled, or the display is not 60Hz, rAF alone
// would emulate slower than realtime and the music would drag: same pitch,
// wrong tempo. So if the ring cannot satisfy the request, this catches up
// before draining, and the machine ends up paced by the sound card.
//
// The catch-up is bounded. A machine that genuinely cannot keep up must degrade
// — dropping emulated frames and letting the audio go short — rather than spend
// ever longer in here trying to buy back time it does not have.
function gbMachine_getSamples(want) {
  // First drain of the session tells the pacing guard in advanceFrame that
  // somebody is listening, and that ring occupancy is now meaningful. Every
  // drain also proves the clock is still alive — see the stall counter there.
  GB_APU_G[GB_AG_ACTIVE] = 1
  gbMachine_pacingStalls = 0

  let n = want
  if (!(n > 0)) {
    // The default AudioStream asks for: roughly two video frames, ~1607 stereo
    // frames at 48kHz.
    n = Math.round(GB_FCTR[GB_F_SAMPLES_PER_FRAME] * 2)
  }
  if (n > GB_AUDIO_OUT_MAX) { n = GB_AUDIO_OUT_MAX }

  if (gbRunning && gbRomLoaded) {
    // A frame emulated here is never the one getFrame presents — the next
    // getFrame emulates and presents its own — so its pixels are work nobody
    // sees. The PPU keeps its whole schedule and leaves them out; see FRAMES
    // NOBODY SEES in gb_ppu.logic. On a machine that cannot hold 60Hz about
    // half the emulated frames come through here, and each is a third cheaper.
    let guard = 0
    GB_PPU_SKIP = 1
    while (GB_APU_G[GB_AG_RING_COUNT] < n && guard < GB_MAX_CATCHUP_FRAMES) {
      if (gbMachine_runSlice(GB_CYCLES_PER_FRAME, 1) === 0) { break }
      guard = guard + 1
    }
    GB_PPU_SKIP = 0
    if (guard > 0) {
      gbFrameCounter = GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME]
      GB_CTR[GB_C_CATCHUP] = GB_CTR[GB_C_CATCHUP] + guard
    }
  }

  return gbMachine_encodeSamples(gbApu_drain(n))
}

// CONTRACT.md §7.3. One byte, active high, whole mask every time — not deltas.
// gb_mmu does the inversion to the DMG's active-low P1 and raises the joypad
// interrupt on a newly-set bit.
function gbMachine_setButtons(mask) {
  let m = mask
  if (!(m >= 0)) { m = 0 }
  gbMmu_setButtons(m & 0xff)
}

// Called once per AudioContext, before the first getSamples().
function gbMachine_setSampleRate(rate) {
  gbState_setSampleRate(rate)
}

// Pause and resume. gbRunning gates advanceFrame, so a paused machine still
// hands back its last picture and still answers getSamples() with silence.
function gbMachine_setRunning(on) {
  if (!gbRomLoaded) {
    gbRunning = false
    return false
  }
  gbRunning = on === true || on === 1
  gbStatus = gbRunning ? (gbTitle === "" ? "Running" : gbTitle) : "Paused"
  return gbRunning
}

// ===========================================================================
// FPS
//
// Measured against the wall clock, not derived from the frame counter, because
// the number's whole job is to show when the machine is NOT keeping up. Only
// frames that actually completed are counted, so a run throttled by the audio
// pacing guard reads 59.7 and not 120.
// ===========================================================================

function gbMachine_now() {
  // `performance` is undefined inside .logic (CONTRACT.md §1.6). Date is not,
  // but this stays defensive: an engine without it should read 0 fps, not throw.
  if (typeof Date === "undefined") { return 0 }
  return Date.now()
}

function gbMachine_measureFps(drew) {
  let now = gbMachine_now()
  if (now === 0) { return }

  if (GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_T0] === 0) {
    GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_T0] = now
    GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N] = 0
    return
  }

  if (drew) {
    GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N] = GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N] + 1
  }

  let elapsed = now - GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_T0]
  if (elapsed >= GB_MACHINE_FPS_WINDOW_MS) {
    let fps = (GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N] * 1000) / elapsed
    GB_FCTR[GB_F_FPS] = fps
    // One decimal, per CONTRACT.md §7.4. Assigned only twice a second, because
    // every write to a plain scalar is a value the state sync deep-compares.
    gbFps = Math.round(fps * 10) / 10
    GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_T0] = now
    GB_FCTR[GB_MACHINE_F_FPS_N] = 0
  }
}

// ===========================================================================
// Save states
//
// One flat byte image, base64'd, in a fixed section order. Everything that is
// already a typed array is copied through a byte view over its own buffer — a
// Uint8Array over an Int32Array's buffer is the same trick gb_state uses for
// GB_AUDIO_OUT_U8, and it means the whole of GB_CTR, GB_CART, GB_RTC and the
// APU serialise with no per-element loop and no endian work.
//
// NOT included, and why:
//
//   * GB_ROM. A state is meaningless without the cart it was taken on, so
//     rather than carry eight megabytes the header carries the sixteen title
//     bytes and the ROM length, and a load refuses a state from another game.
//   * GB_ERAM beyond GB_CART[GB_K_RAMSIZE]. The array is always allocated at
//     the 128KB MBC5 maximum; only the live window is real.
//   * GB_AUDIO_RING and GB_FCTR. The ring is a transport buffer, not machine
//     state — restoring it would replay up to 170ms of stale audio. The sample
//     clock in GB_FCTR belongs to the host's AudioContext, not to the machine,
//     and a state carried between two machines at different rates must not
//     drag the old rate along with it.
//   * GB_TILECACHE. Reconstructible; the loader dirties every row instead.
// ===========================================================================

function gbMachine_ramBytes() {
  let n = GB_CART[GB_K_RAMSIZE]
  if (!(n > 0)) { return 0 }
  if (n > GB_ERAM.length) { return GB_ERAM.length }
  return n
}

// The exact size of a state for the cart currently inserted. Derived from the
// arrays themselves so that a change to gb_state.logic cannot leave a stale
// constant behind — save and load both size themselves from this.
function gbMachine_stateBytes() {
  return GB_MACHINE_STATE_HEADER +
    GB_REG.length +
    GB_REG16.byteLength +
    GB_CPUF.length +
    GB_CTR.byteLength +
    GB_CART.byteLength +
    GB_RTC.byteLength +
    GB_RTC_EPOCH.byteLength +
    GB_PAD.length +
    GB_IO.length +
    GB_HRAM.length +
    GB_OAM.length +
    GB_WRAM.length +
    GB_VRAM.length +
    GB_CGB_BGPAL.length +
    GB_CGB_OBJPAL.length +
    GB_APU.byteLength +
    GB_APU_G.byteLength +
    GB_WAVERAM.length +
    GB_FB.length +
    GB_FB_PRESENT.length +
    gbMachine_ramBytes()
}

// The raw bytes of any typed array, aliasing it rather than copying it. Passed
// byteOffset and byteLength explicitly so this stays correct if gb_state.logic
// ever makes one of these arrays a view into a shared buffer, the way
// GB_AUDIO_OUT_U8 already is.
function gbMachine_byteView(a) {
  return new Uint8Array(a.buffer, a.byteOffset, a.byteLength)
}

function gbMachine_putBytes(dst, offset, src) {
  dst.set(src, offset)
  return offset + src.length
}

function gbMachine_getBytes(src, offset, dst) {
  dst.set(src.subarray(offset, offset + dst.length))
  return offset + dst.length
}

// -> base64 string, or "" with gbError set.
function gbMachine_saveState() {
  gbError = ""
  if (!gbRomLoaded || GB_ROM === null) {
    gbError = "There is no cartridge to save"
    return ""
  }

  // GB_CTR[GB_C_TIMER_BIT] is a mirror of (DIV & mask) that gb_machine's quiet
  // span is allowed to leave behind between edges; freshen it so what goes into
  // the image is the bit as of this instant. Nothing else here is derived.
  gbTimer_syncLive()
  // The CPU flags and DIV live in plain numbers at runtime; the arrays are the
  // save form. See gb_cpu.logic and gb_timer.logic.
  gbCpu_syncFlagsOut()
  gbTimer_syncOut()

  let ramBytes = gbMachine_ramBytes()
  let buf = new Uint8Array(gbMachine_stateBytes())
  let view = new DataView(buf.buffer)

  buf[0] = 0x50 // P
  buf[1] = 0x4b // K
  buf[2] = 0x53 // S
  buf[3] = GB_MACHINE_STATE_VERSION
  buf[4] = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] === 1 ? 1 : 0
  buf[5] = 0
  view.setUint32(6, ramBytes, true)
  view.setUint32(10, GB_ROM.length, true)
  // The cartridge fingerprint: the sixteen header title bytes, raw.
  for (let i = 0; i < 16; i = i + 1) {
    buf[14 + i] = GB_ROM[0x134 + i]
  }
  // 30..31 reserved, already zero.

  let o = GB_MACHINE_STATE_HEADER
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_REG)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_REG16))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_CPUF)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_CTR))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_CART))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_RTC))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_RTC_EPOCH))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_PAD)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_IO)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_HRAM)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_OAM)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_WRAM)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_VRAM)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_CGB_BGPAL)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_CGB_OBJPAL)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_APU))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_APU_G))
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_WAVERAM)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_FB)
  o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_FB_PRESENT)
  if (ramBytes > 0) {
    o = gbMachine_putBytes(buf, o, GB_ERAM.subarray(0, ramBytes))
  }

  return buf.toBase64()
}

// -> true on success. On failure gbError says why and the machine is untouched
// as far as the header checks can manage: everything that can be rejected is
// rejected before the first byte is written back.
function gbMachine_loadState(s) {
  gbError = ""
  if (!gbRomLoaded || GB_ROM === null) {
    gbError = "Insert a cartridge before loading a state"
    return false
  }
  if (typeof s !== "string" || s.length === 0) {
    gbError = "There is no save state to load"
    return false
  }

  let src = null
  try {
    src = gbMachine_decodeBase64(s)
  } catch (error) {
    gbError = "Could not decode that save state: " + error
    return false
  }

  if (src.length < GB_MACHINE_STATE_HEADER) {
    gbError = "That save state is truncated"
    return false
  }
  if (src[0] !== 0x50 || src[1] !== 0x4b || src[2] !== 0x53) {
    gbError = "That is not a Pocket save state"
    return false
  }
  if (src[3] !== GB_MACHINE_STATE_VERSION) {
    gbError = "That save state is version " + src[3] + "; this build reads version " +
      GB_MACHINE_STATE_VERSION
    return false
  }

  let view = new DataView(src.buffer, src.byteOffset, src.length)
  let ramBytes = view.getUint32(6, true)
  let romLength = view.getUint32(10, true)

  if (romLength !== GB_ROM.length || ramBytes !== gbMachine_ramBytes()) {
    gbError = "That save state was taken on a different cartridge"
    return false
  }
  for (let i = 0; i < 16; i = i + 1) {
    if (src[14 + i] !== GB_ROM[0x134 + i]) {
      gbError = "That save state was taken on a different cartridge"
      return false
    }
  }
  if (src.length !== gbMachine_stateBytes()) {
    gbError = "That save state is the wrong length for this cartridge"
    return false
  }

  let o = GB_MACHINE_STATE_HEADER
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_REG)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_REG16))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_CPUF)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_CTR))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_CART))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_RTC))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_RTC_EPOCH))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_PAD)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_IO)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_HRAM)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_OAM)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_WRAM)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_VRAM)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_CGB_BGPAL)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_CGB_OBJPAL)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_APU))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, gbMachine_byteView(GB_APU_G))
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_WAVERAM)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_FB)
  o = gbMachine_getBytes(src, o, GB_FB_PRESENT)
  if (ramBytes > 0) {
    GB_ERAM.fill(0)
    GB_ERAM.set(src.subarray(o, o + ramBytes))
    o = o + ramBytes
  }

  // gbTimer_live and gbMmu_busy were derived from the arrays this function has
  // just overwritten. Neither holds anything of its own, so neither is in the
  // state image; both are re-derived from what came back.
  // The runtime scalars over GB_CPUF and GB_CTR first — gbTimer_syncLive
  // derives its two from DIV, so DIV has to be back before it runs.
  gbCpu_syncFlagsIn()
  gbTimer_syncIn()
  gbTimer_syncLive()
  gbMmu_syncBusy()
  gbMmu_syncIrq()
  GB_PPU_FRAME = GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME]
  // Both framebuffers came back whole, so the frame under construction is not
  // a skipped one whatever was true when the state was taken.
  gbPpu_skipped = 0

  // GB_VRAM went in behind gb_mmu's back, so every tile row is stale — this is
  // case 4 of CONTRACT.md §4, and skipping it is the failure mode that document
  // calls the worst possible one: a picture that is subtly, intermittently
  // wrong rather than obviously broken.
  gbState_invalidateTileCache()

  // The bank registers just changed under the mapper; the precomputed offsets
  // in GB_CART came from the state, but remapping is idempotent and costs
  // nothing here, and it is the documented contract for any bank-register write.
  gbCart_remap()

  // GB_C_PAL_REV came back with GB_CTR, so force the canvas to notice by
  // rebuilding — buildPalette bumps the revision only if the bytes moved, and
  // after a state load they almost always have.
  gbPpu_buildPalette()

  // Transport state, deliberately not restored: start the audio clock clean
  // rather than replaying whatever was queued when the state was taken.
  GB_APU_G[GB_AG_RING_WRITE] = 0
  GB_APU_G[GB_AG_RING_READ] = 0
  GB_APU_G[GB_AG_RING_COUNT] = 0
  GB_AUDIO_RING.fill(0)
  GB_FCTR[GB_F_SAMPLE_ACC] = 0

  // And the machine clock: a state resumes from a whole instruction boundary,
  // never mid-slice.
  GB_CTR[GB_C_CYCLE_BUDGET] = 0

  gbGbcMode = GB_CPUF[GB_P_GBC] === 1
  gbFrameCounter = GB_CTR[GB_C_FRAME]
  gbMachine_resetFpsClock()
  gbStatus = gbTitle === "" ? "Running" : gbTitle
  return true
}
